\par 2.1 Ammount - the number of generated prominences.
\par 2.2 Promin_Radius -this is the radius of the surface from which the prominences arise.
\par 
\par 2.3 FrameRate - the speed of playing the animation of prominences (the number of frames per second).
\par 
\par 2.4 SizeOverLife - this curve gives an indication of the prominence of what size they have at each interval of their life. 
\par By clicking on the curve, you can change it, create your own or change the existing one, I recommend only moving the midpoint of the curve up or down, so the result is the most realistic.
\par 
\par 2.5 ColorOverLife is an HDR gradient that allows you to set the color of prominences at every moment of their life. Click on the gradient and you can modify it.
\par 
\par 2.6 HDR gradient editor:
\par The left edge of the gradient is the birth of a prominence, and the right edge of the gradient is its death.
\par Leave Blend mode is more realistic.
\par the upper sliders above the gradient are responsible for transparency, you can change the Alpha of the click field on the slider and see the effect.
\par The lower sliders are the colors, change them to your comfort, but if you need a yellow sun, it is better to leave it as it is :-)
\par The location moves the selected slider.
\par Below you will see gradient presets, in particular for the blue star.
\par 
\par 2.7 Lifetime random from - the minimum number of the lifetime of a prominence in seconds
\par 
\par 2.8 Lifetime random to - maximum number of prominence lifetime.
\par 
\par 2.9 Trail spawn rate - the number of generated trails
\par 
\par 2.10 Trail gradient see point 2.6 of this document.
\par 
\par 2.11 Trail radius is the distance from the surface of the star that trails can fly away.
\par 
\par 2.12 Trail size - trail size by thickness :-)
\par 
\par 2.13 Trail size over life - this curve gives an indication of the trails of what size they have at each interval of their life. 
\par By clicking on the curve, you can change it, create your own or change the existing one, I recommend only moving the midpoint of the curve up or down, so the result is the most realistic.
\par 
\par 3.Sun Halo
